BERT-Guided Stylistic Hermeneutics (BGSH): A Cross-Translation Statistical Framework for Pauline Interpolation Detection

Johannes Foulds

Department of Computing

University of London

London, United Kingdom

jir2@student.london.ac.uk

###### Abstract

This study introduces BERT-Guided Stylistic Hermeneutics (BGSH), an unsupervised computational framework leveraging masked language models (MLMs) to detect stylistic anomalies within the Pauline epistles. Specifically, DeBERTa models fine-tuned on undisputed Pauline letters compute pseudo-perplexity scores and translation-specific z-score normalisation across six English Bible translations (ESV, NLT, NASB1995, NRSV, NIV, and NET). This novel integration of NLP techniques systematically identifies verses exhibiting stylistic deviations potentially indicative of redaction or interpolation without requiring manual annotations or doctrinal inputs.

**Translation-specific analyses reveal that formal-equivalence translations (e.g., NASB1995) yield greater precision, whereas dynamic translations (e.g., NLT) offer broader anomaly detection coverage. Cross-translation consensus methods--including strict intersection and Stouffer's z-score aggregation--effectively mitigate translation-specific artefacts, significantly increasing the interpretive robustness of anomaly detection. Forward subset selection with penalised threshold optimisation further refines detection, achieving an optimal ensemble configuration (NLT, NRSV, NASB1995, NIV) that surfaces anomalies consistently aligned with longstanding textual-critical hypotheses, such as contested passages in 1 Timothy and 2 Corinthians.**

Although classification metrics, particularly the Matthews Correlation Coefficient (MCC), appear low due to heuristic dataset limitations--reflecting interpretive rather than computational challenges--the model consistently identifies stylistically plausible interpolations recognised in scholarly discourse. The BGSH framework thus offers a reproducible and statistically rigorous approach, enhancing traditional redactional criticism through computational stylometry. Future research directions include direct analysis of the original Koine Greek texts and extension to broader diachronic corpora.

