### _Perplexity Distribution Diagnostics_

To assess the suitability of z-score-based anomaly detection, training perplexity distributions for each translation were examined via quantile-quantile (Q-Q) plots. These plots compare empirical pseudo-perplexity quantiles to a theoretical normal distribution, highlighting deviations from Gaussian assumptions. The goal was to verify whether applying standardised z-score thresholds consistently across translations was statistically sound.

Table II summarises observed distribution characteristics. While most translations showed approximate linearity within the interquartile range, significant deviations were evident in distribution tails. Particularly severe were the right-tailed distributions for NASB1995 and NRSV, reaching maximum perplexities exceeding 3500 and 800 respectively--clear violations of Gaussian assumptions. Translations such as NET and NIV also displayed pronounced upper-tail inflation, whereas ESV and NLT exhibited better overall conformity but still diverged noticeably at higher quantiles.

These findings question the interpretative robustness of z-score normalisation across translations. Although computationally consistent, the statistical interpretation of z-scores varies with distribution shape. Heavy-tailed distributions (NASB1995, NRSV) inflate standard deviations, potentially obscuring moderate anomalies or causing excessive flagging of outliers.

To address these concerns, a robust alternative using the median absolute deviation (MAD) was tested. This replaces mean-based standardisation with median and MAD:

\[z_{\text{robust}}=\frac{x-\text{median}}{1.4826\cdot\text{MAD}}\]

Empirical evaluation, however, showed no performance improvement using this robust measure compared to standard z-scores. Consequently, the standard z-score formulation was retained as a pragmatic heuristic baseline, explicitly acknowledging its statistical limitations.

In summary, the fixed z \(\geq\) 2.0 threshold employed here serves primarily as an initial ranking heuristic rather than a rigorous statistical quantile. The observed variability across translation distributions motivates the subsequent use of cross-translation consensus and threshold optimisation strategies, detailed in Chapter IV.

### _Heuristically-Derived Interpolation Labels Based on Scholarly Annotation_

To enable quantitative evaluation of stylistic anomaly detection, a dataset of proposed interpolations in Pauline epistles was compiled from existing scholarly literature. This structured dataset aggregates historically identified interpolations, providing a basis for comparing automated anomaly detection results against established textual-critical hypotheses.

The dataset includes 296 verses from across the Pauline corpus, annotated with bibliographic metadata and scholarly commentary. It does not represent manually validated ground truth but functions as a heuristic supervisory reference derived from historical-critical scholarship. Consequently, its role is not definitive verification but interpretive alignment assessment for model outputs.

Each verse in the dataset is tagged with metadata, notably scholarly support level (strong, medium, or weak). Strong support indicates widespread scholarly agreement cited in multiple critical commentaries; medium denotes moderate but not unanimous support; weak reflects claims limited to fewer sources or isolated scholarly positions. Inclusion of verses with weak support preserves comprehensive epistemic breadth, enabling sensitivity assessment across widely accepted and more controversial

