### _Fixed-Threshold Evaluation Against Heuristic Labels_

To establish baseline performance, each translation-specific model was evaluated against the heuristic interpolation labels described in Section D. This evaluation utilised a fixed pseudo-perplexity z-score threshold of 2.0 across all translations, without threshold tuning or cross-translation aggregation.

The evaluation dataset consisted of verses from Pauline epistles previously identified as potential interpolations based on scholarly literature. Compiled from over 100 critical sources, these verses were chosen for exhibiting text-critical irregularities, stylistic discontinuities, theological incongruities, or structural interruptions. Although this heuristic dataset is not a definitive benchmark, it provides an interpretive reference set to explore the alignment between automated detections and scholarly debates. Thus, metrics like F1-score and MCC should be viewed as indicative rather than definitive.

Performance was quantified using standard metrics including F1-score, recall, AUC, MCC, and macro-average F1. Additionally, a custom interpretive metric penalising false positives (TP - \(\lambda\cdot\text{FP}\), with \(\lambda\) = 0.55) was applied to assess the balance between sensitivity and specificity, though without threshold optimisation at this stage.

